{"type":"text","timestamp":1793650706634,"sessionID":"ses_e6217deedffeDJw4FrT6AKY9xb","part":{"id":"prt_19de99fee001SaMY7EJTP2B4r0_text-0","sessionID":"ses_e6217deedffeDJw4FrT6AKY9xb","messageID":"msg_19de99fee001SaMY7EJTP2B4r0","type":"text","text":"Done. Deliverables: `./dossier.md` and `./result.json`.\n\n**[action]** Pulled the official IPCA series straight from IBGE SIDRA (table 1737, variable 2265, 12-month accumulated — December value = calendar-year IPCA) via https://apisidra.ibge.gov.br/values/t/1737/p/198501-199612/v/2265/n1/1, and launch dates from each plan's Wikipedia entry. Applied the rubric's formula exactly.\n\n**[result]** Ranking: **Real 0.994, Collor I 0.582, Collor II 0.422**, then Bresser 0.095, Cruzado 0.075, Verão 0.070. Cruzado, Bresser and Verão score zero on the drop — inflation in year Y+1 was *higher* than in Y-1 in all three cases. Only the Real broke hyperinflation permanently (2477.15% in 1993 → 22.41% in 1995, still in force, capped at 60 months by the rubric).\n\n**Assumptions stated:** annual IPCA = December 12-month accumulated value from the IBGE's own series (legacy secondary sources quote 59.9% for 1986 and 480.2% for 1991 due to weight revisions; I use one consistent IBGE source, and the ranking is insensitive to that choice); whole months truncated between launch dates; Collor II dated 31 Jan 1991, Verão 15 Jan 1989.\n\n**[next]** None — brief is complete. How I know it's right: every IPCA number is read directly from the live IBGE SIDRA API response quoted above, every date has a cited URL, and the dossier includes a defensibility section showing the ranking survives both alternative conventions. The one contestable judgment (Collor I's confiscation counting as a \"drop\" when inflation was still 472.70% a year later) is the rubric's arithmetic, flagged in the dossier.","time":{"start":1793650696186,"end":1793650706634}}}

